 never treated as absent. The same filtering result serves the post-merge and range-sweep routes. Its refusal states that the mint was refused while the merge stands and directs recovery through a fetch followed by a sweep over the same range, or through a reviewed repair followed by a sweep whose after revision is the repairing commit. The contract covers ledger and snapshot inputs produced by supported signing and act routes; hand-edited carriers, alias spellings and sub-second timing windows are outside it. \r\n\r\nLLR-158 The one comparison basis an amendment is measured by \r\nDETAIL: An approval that records what it blessed by COPYING the registries needs no canonical text to hash, no separator that cannot occur in a cell and no second exclusion list, so no digest engine sits behind this row � the comparison basis is discharged by split_changed_cells(csv_path, id_col, head, row) -> {\"approved\": {cell: (before, after)}, \"traced\": {...}}: the id column and Status are excluded STRUCTURALLY - the id is the join key rather than content, and folding Status in would make every flip read as an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip - and the remainder is split by spine_cell_class, the SAME classifier the amend-without-flip guard reads, so the two can never disagree about what normative means. It is PUBLIC for exactly one reason: baseline_snapshot.is_drifted reads it as the drift basis (LLR-173), so the snapshot comparison and the staged-amendment guard are one rule with one home rather than two rules that agree until they do not. _APPROVED_TEXT names the one Status value whose row TEXT is approved (Approved � the status fold collapsed Verified and Planned into it) and requires the SAME value on both sides, so prose rewritten under an Approved row is never invisible. It returns the before/after PAIRS a brief has to render anyway, which is why it replaced a hash rather than being wrapped by one. Needs are compared row by row through the same basis, their approved and traced cells split like every other tier's. ONE TWO-TREE WALK feeds every reader that asks what a spine delta did: _spine_row_sides pairs each row's before and after side once, and four consumers read it � staged_spine_amendments (the rows whose approved text moved), staged_approval_acts (the rows that crossed INTO an approval claim, or arrived already making one), staged_drafted_rows (the rows a lane authored below approval, which the first-approval mint is raised over) and lane_approval_refusal, which words the merge slot's approval-act refusals. The walk is shared rather than copied so that the set one reader EXEMPTS is the set another REPORTS, MINUS the de-approvals: an Approved -> Drafted withdrawal moves Status, so the amendment reader exempts it, but it blesses nothing, so the approval-act reader does not report it and staged_drafted_rows raises the re-approval it now owes instead. Subject to that one subtraction, an approval act is invisible to the amendment reader by construction, and a row that no reader saw is unrepresentable. The per-row judgement is stated once, in _approval_act, so the flip arm and the born arm are read side by side rather than as two branches of the walk that could drift apart. UNREPRESENTABLE WITHIN A DECLARED BOUND, and the bound is named rather than left as an absence � the walk's universe is a PARAMETER over two declared sets, not one set every reader shares. SPINE_CSVS is the THREE registries whose rows carry an id column and an approved text � system-requirements, low-level-requirements, test-cases � and is the walk's default, so staged_drafted_rows, and the first-approval mint over it, read exactly it. APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS is those three PLUS stakeholder-needs and the assumptions registry's two tiers (assumptions and surrogates), and staged_approval_acts passes it, so the merge slot requires each approval act, including one on a need, to have an accepted verdict that judges its own rows. The need tier is inside because it carries the same status vocabulary and every approval act requires the same independent verdict. An unreadable claimed frontmatter is refused by name at the merge slot and at partial close rather than read as an unscoped lane; a lane that records no scope is judged only by its acted rows and copies, with no `Adjudicates` wording. AMENDMENT_CSVS is an alias of APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS rather than a second list, and staged_spine_amendments passes it, so the amend-without-flip warn and the intake amendment mint, which both consume that walk, cover the need, assumption and surrogate tiers beside the three; the warn's Hat-Refs arm stays structurally silent on those three tiers, none of which carries Hat-Refs. The mint additionally drops records the merged range already settled under LLR-364, while the pre-commit warn reads the unfiltered walk because it runs before any act. The three registries a snapshot anchors that no approval reader walks � interfaces, external, components � are listed in OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT: off-spine, their approval cells governed by their own rung rather than this one. The two lists are pinned as one exhaustive, disjoint statement, SNAPSHOTTED == APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS + OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT, against baseline_snapshot.SNAPSHOTTED's eight. A tier added to the snapshot therefore lands on one side or the other by a deliberate edit, and cannot reach no approval reader at all. \r\n\r\nLLR-271 The needs file's two tiers compared with their recorded copy \r\nDETAIL: baseline_snapshot.NEED_TIERS names the needs file's need tier (SN-ID) and stakeholder tier (STK-ID), and SNAPSHOT_TIERS lists both, so every compared-tier reader walks them; load_all reads both off the record under the same column names as the live rows, so is_drifted asks drift of a need or a stakeholder exactly as of any other row. owing_rows(rel, id_col, rows, snapshot_rows) returns (id, why, cells, row) for each row owing an act - a Drafted row, a row with no Status cell, and a row claiming approval whose approved cells moved from its recorded copy, with those cells' before and after - and is the one owing rule, which the assumption section reads as well. needs_owing runs it over NEED_TIERS. trace.need_brief_lines renders a section of the re-attestation brief, outside any block the approval dial collapses, with one block per owing row: a drifted row's moved cells before and after, any other row's cells in full; nothing when none owes, and the brief's freshness window stays open while one does. _approved_by_act counts NEED_TIERS among the compared tiers, so an act carrying a need or a stakeholder into approval names it in the act ledger. The refresh refusal holds an act copying the needs registry while a need or stakeholder drifted unread, and --reattests naming it re-anchors it (LLR-245); the unanchored rule reports an approved one absent from its copy or recorded below approval (LLR-173). tier_owing is the generic form of needs_owing: it applies owing_rows to an explicit ordered set of tiers for readers, including the amendment brief's need, assumption and surrogate rows. The owner's open-items page lists the same owing need and stakeholder rows as the re-attestation brief: a drifted row has its moved cells before and after, and a first-approval row has its cells in full. \r\n\r\nLLR-273 Each registry's own recorded copy named on the owner's and the judge's surfaces \r\nDETAIL: baseline_snapshot.registry_stamps(root) returns (registry, short rev, date) for each registry the record holds a committed copy of, from stamp(root, registry). The re-attestation brief's baseline lines (trace._baseline_lines) name each such copy and its commit, every line carrying the derived-stamp prefix the freshness check drops; the open-items view lists the same pairs; reattest_model's entries carry `baselines` (trace._spine_stamps), the stamp of each spine registry's own copy, with `baseline` the requirement registry's; the off-spine census counts rulings since each registry's own copy; and the first-approval brief's anchor (_copy_stamp_lines, shared with the amendment brief) names the commit of each registry the act copies. \r\n\r\nLLR-278 An adjudication re-attestation constrained at merge \r\nDETAIL: acceptance_record.merge_approval_refusal applies the additional adjudication bounds whenever any claimed row records a scope, including a mixed lane, and calls reattest_scope_refusal for that scope-bound lane. A malformed combined token is refused by name; an act-free delta has no scope refusal. reattested_between reads the ledger at the merge base and the head and takes the ids re-attested by the entries whose seq the base does not hold, refusing by name when either side does not parse. Every such id outside amendment_scope - the union of the claimed amendment rows' own amendment scope, including their amendment:<id> tokens when a combined sitting claims them, empty when none is claimed - is refused by name before the first-approval judgement runs. Every approval act in the lane�s complete acted set - a live flip or born-approved row, a ledger-only approval, or a re-attestation - is refused when any row it approves lacks an APPROVE ruling in an accepted first-approval part or any row it re-attests lacks a CLARITY ruling, or a MEANING ruling carrying the bless mark, in an accepted amendment part among the branch's bindings, whether or not the act names a verdict; a named verdict must also authorize every row its act re-attests in the same way, and acts already on trunk are not re-read. An act that both approves rows and re-attests rows, claimed either by two single-kind rows or by one combined sitting's per-kind tokens, merges when each half lies inside its claiming row's own kind scope, whether their rows are in one registry or different ones; an id under another combined kind authorizes neither half, a snapshot is WIDENED only when the complete acted set holds no approved or re-attested row in that registry identity, a row the act ledger names being placed in the registry its id's tier names whether or not the row remains in the live registry at the head, so a retirement authorizes its own registry's copy: every carrier path of an authorized registry, including a deleted obsolete carrier copy, is authorized, while an unauthorized registry's copy is WIDENED on every carrier path. \r\n\r\nLLR-359 A held re-attestation carries the text identity its verdict judged \r\nDETAIL: The approved-text digest is formed from a row's nonempty approved cells, excluding its id and Status, so carrier representation and an absent versus empty cell give the same identity. An amendment brief renders one judged-text identity line for each shown row. Reservation writes those identities in the binding, and rewriting the binding as accepted or failed preserves them. A held CLARITY re-attestation is actable only when the accepted binding's identity equals the committed row's approved-text digest; an absent, malformed or unequal identity yields a row-naming refusal before launch. \r\n\r\nLLR-361 Act-ledger entries record the registry files an act copied \r\nDETAIL: An act-ledger entry written by a snapshot records `copied` as the non-empty sorted distinct canonical paths of the snapshot registry files that act wrote. A legacy entry has no copy set, which reads as unknown rather than empty and is not inferred. An entry a commit adds relative to each parent carries `copied` naming only snapshot registry files. An entry a commit adds relative to each parent without `copied`, or with `copied` naming a file outside the snapshot's registry files, is refused. A whole ledger refuses an entry that omits `copied` after an entry that carries it. A malformed parent or hand-edited ledger lies outside this commit-bound reading. A copied file does not establish that its rows were judged. \r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
